<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-10-11">
  <title>Week 10 and Week 11</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      These weeks made the idea of a limit precise and then put it to work.
      We stated the definition of a limit in terms of an error tolerance <m>\epsilon</m> for the output and a precision <m>\delta</m> for the input, and we used particular values of <m>\epsilon</m> to see when the definition is satisfied.
      From the definition we built limit rules and used them to justify limits of formulas, one step at a time.
      We then used tangent lines to define the derivative function and learned to sketch the graph of the derivative from the graph of the original function.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>L4 Illustrating the Limit Definition</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-L4">
      <title>L4</title>
      <p>
        I can illustrate the definition of a limit as being satisfied or not satisfied using particular values for epsilon.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      What do I need to understand?
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m> means that for every <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m> there exists some <m>\delta \gt 0</m> so that <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m> whenever <m>0 \lt |x-a| \lt \delta</m>.
            The value <m>\epsilon</m> is an acceptable tolerance of error for the output, and <m>\delta</m> is how close the input must be to <m>a</m> to guarantee that tolerance.
            Both are distances, so in a context they carry the units of the output and the input.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The inequality <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m> means <m>L-\epsilon \lt f(x) \lt L+\epsilon</m>, a horizontal strip around the line <m>y=L</m>.
            Solving this inequality for <m>x</m> gives the set of inputs whose outputs are acceptable.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For a particular <m>\epsilon</m>, the definition is <em>satisfied</em> when the set of acceptable inputs contains an interval around <m>x=a</m>, with the point <m>x=a</m> itself allowed to be left out.
            Then <m>\delta</m> is the smaller of the two distances from <m>a</m> to the endpoints of that interval, and any smaller positive number also works.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For a particular <m>\epsilon</m>, the definition is <em>not satisfied</em> when the set of acceptable inputs does not reach all the way around <m>x=a</m>.
            For example, <m>a</m> may lie outside the set, or on its edge.
            Then no positive <m>\delta</m> works, and the limit statement is false.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            One value of <m>\epsilon</m> that fails is enough to show a limit statement is false.
            One value of <m>\epsilon</m> that works is not enough to show it is true, since the definition requires a working <m>\delta</m> for <em>every</em> <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>


    <exercise label="L4-checkpoint">
      <title>L4</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A circuit is powered by a 12 volt battery connected across a resistor of <m>R</m> ohms.
          The current in amperes is <m>I = \dfrac{12}{R}</m>.
          With <m>R=4</m> ohms the current is exactly 3 amperes.
          The device needs the current to be within <m>0.2</m> amperes of 3 amperes.
          How much can the resistance vary from 4 ohms?
          Explain how this relates to the definition of a limit.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The current must satisfy <m>|I-3| \lt 0.2</m>, so <m>2.8 \lt I \lt 3.2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We need <m>2.8 \lt \dfrac{12}{R} \lt 3.2</m> with <m>R \gt 0</m>.
          Taking reciprocals reverses the inequalities, and multiplying by 12 gives <m>\dfrac{12}{3.2} \lt R \lt \dfrac{12}{2.8}</m>, which is <m>3.75 \lt R \lt \dfrac{30}{7} \approx 4.2857</m>. Alternatively, we find the endpoints by solving <m>\dfrac{12}{R} = 2.8</m> and <m>\dfrac{12}{R} = 3.2</m> and use those points as the endpoints of the interval.
        </p>
        <p>
          The lower endpoint is <m>4-3.75=0.25</m> below 4 ohms and the upper endpoint is <m>\frac{30}{7}-4=\frac{2}{7}\approx 0.2857</m> above 4 ohms.
          The symmetric neighborhood <m>|R-4| \lt \delta</m> fits inside the acceptable interval when <m>\delta \le 0.25</m>, so <m>\delta = 0.25</m> is the largest value that works, and any smaller positive value also works.
        </p>
        <p>
          The situation illustrates <m>\displaystyle \lim_{R \to 4} \frac{12}{R} = 3</m> for the tolerance <m>\epsilon = 0.2</m>.
          If <m>0 \lt |R-4| \lt 0.25</m>, then <m>\left|\dfrac{12}{R}-3\right| \lt 0.2</m>.
          Here <m>\epsilon=0.2</m> is the tolerance on the output current in amperes and <m>\delta=0.25</m> is the precision on the input resistance in ohms.
          The definition of the limit makes the stronger promise that a working <m>\delta</m> exists for <em>every</em> tolerance <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>, not just <m>0.2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>L5 Justifying Limits with Limit Rules</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-L5">
      <title>L5</title>
      <p>
        I can apply limit rules according to rules of deductive reasoning to justify the value of a limit based on formulas.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      What do I need to understand?
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A justification is a sequence of true limit statements, each followed by the reason it is true.
            The reason is either a named rule (an elementary limit or a limit of a combination) or <q>given</q>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Deconstruct the formula to plan the justification: find the last operation, then repeat for each piece until every piece is a constant, the identity function, or a linear function.
            Write the justification in the opposite order, starting from those elementary pieces and building up to the full formula.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each combination rule requires that the limits of its parts are already known.
            In each step, name the earlier step numbers that you are combining.
            Powers of <m>x</m> are justified by repeated use of the product rule.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The quotient rule and the reciprocal rule require that the limit of the denominator is <em>not zero</em>.
            Write down the limit of the denominator and note that it is nonzero before using the rule.
            If it is zero, the rule cannot be used and a different method, such as factoring and cancelling, is needed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A given limit such as <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} g(x) = M</m> is a place to start, with the reason <q>given</q>.
            It does not say that <m>g(a)=M</m>, and <m>g(a)</m> may not even exist. We may also be given a limit of some combination, such as <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} \frac{g(x)}{x-a}</m>, which cannot be separated since division by <m>x-a</m> would have denominator 0.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <exercise label="L5-checkpoint">
      <title>L5</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>f(x)</m> is a function for which <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} f(x)(x-4) = 5</m>.
          Find and justify the limit
          <md>
            \lim_{x \to 4} \frac{2f(x)(x-4)}{x^2-2x+3}
          </md>
          using limit rules involving either elementary limits or combinations of limits that have already been demonstrated.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The last operation is a quotient.
          The numerator is a constant multiple of the product <m>f(x)(x-4)</m>, whose limit is given, so we treat that product as a single piece and do not need to know <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} f(x)</m> by itself.
          The denominator is built from <m>x^2</m>, <m>2x</m>, and a constant.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} f(x)(x-4) = 5</m> (given).</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} 2f(x)(x-4) = 2(5) = 10</m> (constant multiple rule with Step 1).</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} x = 4</m> (limit of the identity function).</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} x^2 = (4)(4) = 16</m> (product rule with Step 3 used for both factors).</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} -2x+3 = -2(4)+3 = -5</m> (limit of a linear function3).</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} (x^2-2x+3) = 16+-5 = 11</m> (sum rule with Steps 4 and 5).</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 4} \frac{2f(x)(x-4)}{x^2-2x+3} = \frac{10}{11}</m> (quotient rule with Steps 2 and 6, which applies because the limit of the denominator, <m>11</m>, is not zero).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>D3 Sketching the Derivative Graph</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-D3">
      <title>D3</title>
      <p>
        I can create (sketch) a graph of a derivative function based on the original function's graph.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      What do I need to understand?
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The output <m>f'(a)</m> is the <em>slope</em> of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m>.
            The graph of <m>f'(x)</m> reports slopes, not heights, so it does not look like a copy of the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Where <m>f(x)</m> has a horizontal tangent line, such as a local maximum or local minimum, <m>f'(x)=0</m>.
            Mark these <m>x</m>-values first as the places where the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> meets the <m>x</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Where <m>f(x)</m> is increasing, the slopes are positive, so the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> is above the <m>x</m>-axis.
            Where <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing, the slopes are negative, so the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> is below the <m>x</m>-axis.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The steeper the graph of <m>f(x)</m>, the farther the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> is from the <m>x</m>-axis.
            Between two horizontal tangents, the graph of <m>f(x)</m> is steepest near an inflection point, so that is where <m>f'(x)</m> reaches a local maximum or local minimum.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Build the sketch in order: mark the zeros, decide the sign on each interval, locate the steepest points, and then connect everything with a smooth curve.
            Estimates of slopes are fine, but the zeros, signs, and the places where <m>f'(x)</m> is largest or smallest must be correct.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <exercise label="D3-checkpoint">
      <title>D3</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is shown for <m>-3 \le x \le 4</m>.
          Each grid square is 1 unit wide and 1 unit tall.
        </p>
        <image label="D3-image-quartic" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            An M-shaped graph for x from -3 to 4 on a grid.
            It rises from about (-3, -0.8) to a local maximum at (-2, 2), falls to a local minimum at (0, 0), rises to a higher local maximum at (3, 5.9), and then falls to (4, 2).
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.6cm, >=stealth]
              \draw[gray!40, xstep=1, ystep=1] (-3,-2) grid (4,7);
              \draw[->] (-3.3,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (0,-2.3) -- (0,7.4) node[above] {$y$};
              \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                \node[below] at (\x,-0.05) {\small $\x$};
              }
              \foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7} {
                \node[left] at (-0.05,\y) {\small $\y$};
              }
              \draw[thick, domain=-3:4, samples=80, smooth]
                plot (\x, {-3*(\x*\x*\x*\x/4 - \x*\x*\x/3 - 3*\x*\x)/8});
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <p>
          Sketch the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> on the axes below.
          Mark the <m>x</m>-values where <m>f'(x)=0</m> and your graph
          should show clearly where <m>f'(x)</m> is increasing and decreasing, 
          with reasonable estimates for actual values at extremes.
        </p>
        <image label="D3-image-quartic-axes" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            Blank axes on a grid for x from -3 to 4 and for slope values from -10 to 8.
            The horizontal scale matches the graph of f(x).
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.3cm, >=stealth]
              \draw[gray!40, xstep=1, ystep=1] (-3,-10) grid (4,8);
              \draw[->] (-3.3,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (0,-10.3) -- (0,8.4) node[above] {$y$};
              \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                \node[below] at (\x,-0.05) {\small $\x$};
              }
              \foreach \y in {-10,-8,-6,-4,-2,2,4,6,8} {
                \node[left] at (-0.05,\y) {\small $\y$};
              }
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The graph of <m>f(x)</m> has horizontal tangent lines at <m>x=-2</m>, <m>x=0</m>, and <m>x=3</m>, so <m>f'(x)=0</m> at those three <m>x</m>-values.
        </p>
        <p>
          The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on <m>(-\infty,-2)</m> and <m>(0,3)</m>, so <m>f'(x)</m> is positive there.
          It is decreasing on <m>(-2,0)</m> and <m>(3,\infty)</m>, so <m>f'(x)</m> is negative there.
          The graph of <m>f'(x)</m> therefore crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at each of the three <m>x</m>-values, and it has the shape of a cubic with a negative leading coefficient.
        </p>
        <p>
          The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is steepest between the horizontal tangent lines near its inflection points, at about <m>x=-1.1</m> and <m>x=1.8</m>.
          On <m>(-2,0)</m> the graph is falling, so <m>f'(x)</m> has a local minimum near <m>x=-1.1</m> with a slope of about <m>-1.5</m>.
          On <m>(0,3)</m> the graph is rising, so <m>f'(x)</m> has a local maximum near <m>x=1.8</m> with a slope of about <m>3</m>.
          The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is rising fairly steeply at <m>x=-3</m> and falling steeply at <m>x=4</m>, so <m>f'(x)</m> is about <m>6.75</m> at <m>x=-3</m> and <m>-9</m> at <m>x=4</m>.
        </p>
        <image label="D3-image-quartic-solution" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            A graph for x from -3 to 4 that crosses the x-axis at x = -2, 0, and 3.
            It is positive and falling for x less than -2, then negative between -2 and 0 with a local minimum near (-1.1, -1.5), then positive between 0 and 3 with a local maximum near (1.8, 3), and then negative and falling for x greater than 3.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm, y=0.3cm, >=stealth]
              \draw[gray!40, xstep=1, ystep=1] (-3,-10) grid (4,8);
              \draw[->] (-3.3,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (0,-10.3) -- (0,8.4) node[above] {$y$};
              \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                \node[below] at (\x,-0.05) {\small $\x$};
              }
              \foreach \y in {-10,-8,-6,-4,-2,2,4,6,8} {
                \node[left] at (-0.05,\y) {\small $\y$};
              }
              \draw[thick, domain=-3:4, samples=80, smooth]
                plot (\x, {-3*(\x+2)*\x*(\x-3)/8});
              \filldraw (-2,0) circle (2.2pt);
              \filldraw (0,0) circle (2.2pt);
              \filldraw (3,0) circle (2.2pt);
              \filldraw (-1.12,-1.523) circle (2.2pt);
              \filldraw (1.79,3.078) circle (2.2pt);
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
